# Offline cell-tower database for rhodep-gnss-daemon's `cell` source.
#
# Read by rhodep-cell-db.service, which is driven by rhodep-cell-db.timer.
# NEITHER IS ENABLED BY DEFAULT, on purpose: a refresh streams the full
# OpenCelliD dump, which is 1.1 GB, and a package should not decide on your
# behalf to pull that over what may be a metered link. Turn it on with
#
#     systemctl enable --now rhodep-cell-db.timer
#
# or just build the database once, by hand:
#
#     rhodep-cell-db build --mcc 722 --source ocid,mls
#
# Neither is required. Without a database the cell source simply starts at the
# network providers instead, which is where it would end up anyway for any cell
# the dumps do not have.
#
# CONFFILE. This file is listed in the package's DEBIAN/conffiles, so dpkg
# tracks whatever you write here: an upgrade that ships a different default
# stops and asks rather than overwriting your edits. That also means an
# upgrade is not silent if you have touched this file, so read the prompt.

# Mobile country codes to index, comma-separated. 722 is Argentina.
# Each extra country costs disk and download time but nothing at query time.
# See https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mobile_country_code
MCC="722"

# Which dumps to merge, in order of preference; the first one named wins where
# they disagree about a cell.
#   ocid  Internet Archive mirror of the OpenCelliD full export, 2024-05-19,
#         CC-BY-SA 4.0. 1.1 GB compressed.
#   mls   Internet Archive copy of the final Mozilla Location Service cell
#         export, 2024-03-27, public domain. 1.6 GB compressed. Frozen for
#         good -- MLS shut down in 2024 -- but it was an independent survey,
#         so it has cells OpenCelliD does not. MEASURED for MCC 722: ocid
#         alone gives 312k cells, ocid+mls gives 653k.
# You can also give a URL or a local .csv/.csv.gz here.
SOURCE="ocid,mls"

# Where to write it. The daemon's --cell-db default is this same path.
DB="/var/lib/rhodep-gnss/cells.sqlite"

# If you have an OpenCelliD API access token, the official per-MCC export is
# downloaded instead of the mirror: far smaller, and current rather than two
# years old. Tokens are free but registration is a web form with a reCAPTCHA,
# so there is no way to obtain one from a script and this package does not
# ship one. https://opencellid.org/
#
# WRITE THE WHOLE FLAG, not just the token. The value of TOKEN_ARG is pasted
# onto the rhodep-cell-db command line by rhodep-cell-db.service as an
# unquoted $TOKEN_ARG, which systemd splits at whitespace, so
#
#     TOKEN_ARG="--token 0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef01234567"
#
# arrives as the two arguments --token and the token, while leaving the line
# commented out or empty arrives as no arguments at all. That is the only way
# a unit file can add an argument only sometimes without pulling in a shell;
# see the comment above ExecStart= in rhodep-cell-db.service. A bare TOKEN=
# does nothing, here or in any earlier version of this file: nothing has ever
# read it.
#
# TWO THINGS THE OFFICIAL EXPORT REQUIRES, both checked against
# rhodep-cell-db's own code rather than assumed:
#   - MCC above must name exactly ONE country. The token endpoint serves one
#     MCC per request and rhodep-cell-db refuses to guess which one you meant.
#   - SOURCE above must name exactly ONE source. A token overrides the source
#     name entirely, so "ocid,mls" with a token downloads the same official
#     export twice and merges it with itself. Set SOURCE="ocid".
#
# The token ends up in the journal, because rhodep-cell-db prints the URL it
# is fetching. It also ends up in /proc/PID/cmdline while the build runs.
# Neither is worse than it sitting in this file, but do not assume it is
# secret once the timer has run.
#
#TOKEN_ARG="--token 0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef01234567"
